2. Method Overriding:
-----------------------
Method overriding in Java occurs when a subclass provides its own implementation of a method that is already defined in its 
superclass. The method in the child class must have the same name, return type, and parameters as the one in the parent class. 
This allows the subclass to tailor or extend the functionality of the inherited method. 

Rules for Method overriding:
---------------------
Same Method Signature: The method name, return type, and parameter list must be exactly the same as in the superclass.

Inheritance is Required: Overriding can only occur between a subclass and its superclass.

Access Modifier Must Not Be More Restrictive: The overriding method cannot have a more restrictive access modifier than the method in the parent class.

Return Type Must Be Same or Covariant: The return type must be the same or a subclass of the original return type (covariant return type).

Final Methods Cannot Be Overridden: A method declared as final in the superclass cannot be overridden in the subclass.

Static Methods Cannot Be Overridden (Only Hidden): Static methods are class-level, and they cannot be overridden; only method hiding applies.

Private Methods Cannot Be Overridden: Private methods are not inherited, hence cannot be overridden.

Constructors Cannot Be Overridden: Constructors are not inherited, and therefore cannot be overridden.

Exception Handling Rules Must Be Followed: The overriding method cannot throw broader checked exceptions than the overridden method.

