Association, Aggregation, and Composition define how objects in OOP relate, ranging from weak links to tight ownership. Association is a generic "uses-a" relationship. 
Aggregation is a "has-a" (aggregation) relationship where parts exist independently of the container. Composition is a strict "part-of" relationship where the child cannot exist without the parent. 
1. Association (Weakest)
Definition: A general relationship between two independent objects, defining how they collaborate.
Nature: Neither object owns the other; both can exist independently.
Example: A Teacher and a Student. They are associated, but one does not own the other.
UML: A simple line connecting two classes. 
2. Aggregation (Medium)
Definition: A special form of association representing a "has-a" relationship, where one object (container) uses another (part).
Nature: The child object can exist independently of the parent container.
Example: A Library and Books. If the library is destroyed, the books still exist.
UML: A line with a hollow diamond on the container side. 
3. Composition (Strongest)
Definition: A strict, restricted form of aggregation where the child object cannot exist without the parent.
Nature: The container owns the lifecycle of the child; if the container is destroyed, the child is destroyed.
Example: A House and its Rooms. If the house is destroyed, the rooms cease to exist as part of it.
UML: A line with a filled (solid) diamond on the container side. 


