Cohesion and Coupling:
-----------------------
1. Cohesion (Intra-module)
Definition: Cohesion refers to the degree to which the elements inside a single module or class are related and focused on a single, well-defined purpose. It is the "internal glue".
What to aim for: High Cohesion. A class should do one thing and do it well (Single Responsibility Principle).
Example of High Cohesion: A UserAuthService class that only handles login, logout, and password reset.
Example of Low Cohesion: A UserUtility class that handles authentication, formats dates, and sends emails. 
2. Coupling (Inter-module)
Definition: Coupling refers to the degree of interdependence between different modules, classes, or components. It measures how closely connected they are.
What to aim for: Low Coupling (Loose Coupling). Modules should function independently, with minimal knowledge of each other's internal workings.
Example of High Coupling (Bad): Module A directly modifies the internal variables of Module B. If B changes, A breaks.
Example of Low Coupling (Good): Module A interacts with Module B only through a well-defined public interface (API). 
